            <ArticleTitle>A rare case of concomitant stress (takotsubo) cardiomyopathy and acute myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>499.e1-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.repc.2015.01.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0870-2551(15)00065-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Stress cardiomyopathy and myocardial infarction are generally regarded as mutually exclusive diagnoses. We report the case of a 54-year-old woman who presented with acute chest pain. Her echocardiogram and ventriculography were typical of stress cardiomyopathy, but she had one subocclusive coronary lesion, a highly significant rise in troponin and a subendocardial myocardial infarction scar documented on cardiac magnetic resonance. This is a rare case of concomitant myocardial infarction and stress cardiomyopathy, in which the acute coronary syndrome itself may have been the stressor, given the absence of other identifiable causes.</AbstractText>
